  "worked_solution": "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\,y & =x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta } & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$",
